    "q-139856": {
      "dom_id": "step-q139856",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "A random sample of size $n=51$ is conducted from a population. It is known that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{51}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=24.5\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere each ${x}_{i}$ is a sample observation. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population standard deviation. Round your answer to two decimal places where appropriate.",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nWe're given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{51}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=24.5$ and $n=51.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{51-1}\\cdot 24.5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\cdot 24.5 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}=0.49.$ We take the square root to compute an unbiased estimate of the standard deviation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\sigma }=\\sqrt{0.49}=0.7.\n$$",
