    "q-139860": {
      "dom_id": "step-q139860",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Suppose that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{100}$ is a random sample of size $n=100$ from a population, and \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=198.\n$$\n\n \n\nCompute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nHere, there are $n=100$ data points in the sample, and we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=198.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{100-1}\\cdot 198 \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\,800}{99} \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
