    "q-88857": {
      "dom_id": "step-q88857",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n4,\\,8,\\,10,\\,6,\\,7\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(4+8+10+6+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{5} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({4}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{10}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{265}{5} \\\\  & =53.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =53-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
